Shawn I Roske wrote:

> I'm wondering what are good things to wish for.  If I wished for a wand
> of wishes I wonder if I would get it?  

Probably yes, but cancelled so it would never work and you couldn't charge
it. (Wand of wishing, by the way, not of wishes),

> I tried dragonscale armor, but 
> the wand gave me a cape of magic resistance. 

That's because it's not "dragonscale armor" but "dragon scale mail". Be
sure to specify the colour: silver dragon scale mail gives reflection,
gray dragon scale mail magic resistance.

(And it's a *cloak* of magic resistance, not a cape).

> I think I just spelled it 
> wrong.  I asked for an amulet of telepathy 

Amulet of ESP.

> and got an amulet of detect 
> invisible, 

??? Never heard of that. There's a ring of see invisible, and a potion of
see invisible, but not an amulet.

> and I wished for a ring of identify and got a ring of 
> not-needing-air or something.  

There's no ring of identify; scrolls and spellbooks, yes. And there's no
ring that makes you not need air; there's an amulet of magical breathing,
and a ring of free action (did you get that? protects against being
paralysed by, for instacne, floating eyes).

> Are wishes generally random? 

Only if you wish for something nonexistent or something the wishing parser
doesn't understand.

> I got  
> stormbringer from a fountain.  Perhaps wands are not as powerful as
> fountains.

Wands are completey reliable as long as they're not cursed, have some
charges, and you know what you're doing.

> I haven't tried explore mode.  Is it worth it to use that mode?

For practice, or if you want to avoid dying, certainly, and you start with
a wand of wishing, but the wishing itself isn't any different.

